      "text_raw": "At a frequency of 100 kHz,\non the other hand, which is\nnot such a high frequency\nfor this amplifier, the gain\nacross the input stage\ndecreases to about unity.\nThe gain of the output stage\nis still approximately 100.\nThe signal levels at the\ninput and at the intermedi-\nate point are shown in this\nslide.\nIt is clear that the distor-\ntion in the input stage will\nnow be much larger,\nwhereas the distortion on\nthe output stage is the same as at 100 Hz.\nThe relative current swing in the input stage has increased by a factor of 100. It is now 0.05.\nThis is still too small to be able to play a role for distortion.\nThe calculations show this.",
      "text": "At a frequency of 100 kHz, on the other hand, which is not such a high frequency for this amplifier, the gain across the input stage decreases to about unity. The gain of the output stage is still approximately 100. The signal levels at the input and at the intermediate point are shown in this slide. It is clear that the distortion in the input stage will now be much larger, whereas the distortion on the output stage is the same as at 100 Hz. The relative current swing in the input stage has increased by a factor of 100. It is now 0.05. This is still too small to be able to play a role for distortion. The calculations show this."
    }
